<body>
<div id="prog"></div>
<header>
  <div class="logo"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt=""> ARRY&rsquo;S <b>ROOFING</b></div>
  <div class="hdr-r">5330 Gulf of Mexico Dr</div>
</header>
<section id="hero">
  <div class="letter">
    <div class="cover-wrap"><img src="https://arrysroofing.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/Giebner2.jpg" alt="5330 Gulf of Mexico Dr"><div class="cover-tag">OUR OWN WORK &middot; CONCEALED FASTENER METAL ON THE WATER</div></div>
    <div class="inner">
      <div class="seal"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Arry's seal"></div>
      <div class="to"></div>
      <h1>Little Gull Cottages &amp; Resort</h1>
      <div class="addr">5330 Gulf of Mexico Dr &middot; Longboat Key, FL 34228</div>
      <p class="body">You already know the thing that makes Little Gull unusual. The property runs shore to shore, Gulf of Mexico across to Sarasota Bay, and the two ends have not aged the same way.</p><p class="body">I inspected the buildings on 15 July 2026. The bayside roofs are fine. The six Gulf-side buildings, eight rental units between them, are ready for replacement, and water is already into the eaves.</p><p class="body">This document puts three prices against that. Same tear-off, same wood repairs, same crew on all three. What changes is the panel, and what the manufacturer will warrant at this address.</p>
      <div class="sig">
        <div class="name">&mdash; Adam Velett &amp; the Arry&rsquo;s Team</div>
        <div class="role">Family roofers of Tampa Bay since 1990</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section><section id="context" class="dark">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="rail rv"><span>0 2 &middot; The Property</span><span class="mid">&middot;</span><span class="t2">Longboat Key &middot; Manatee County</span></div>
    <h1 class="h1 rv d1">Same roofs. Different weather.</h1>
    <p class="sub rv d1">Why the bayside cottages are fine and the Gulf-side cottages are not.</p>
    <p class="body rv d2">Your buildings sit about a mile from the open Gulf, on a barrier island roughly 65 miles down the road from our Tarpon Springs shop. Longboat Key straddles the Manatee and Sarasota line, and 5330 Gulf of Mexico Drive falls on the Manatee side. That address is the most important fact in this document.</p><p class="body rv d2">The bayside cottages and the Gulf-side cottages went on at the same time, in the same material, by the same hands. One set is sound. The other is corroding through. Nothing about age or workmanship explains that. Exposure does, and the controlled experiment is sitting on your own property.</p><p class="body rv d2">It is also why the warranty answer changes by product. Manufacturers write coastal coverage by setback from breaking surf, not by county line. A panel approved with no setback along most of the Gulf Coast can still be excluded at a beachfront address like this one.</p>
    <figure class="shot rv d2"><img src="https://arrysroofing.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/404Bay1.jpg" alt="A metal roof installed by Arry&rsquo;s Roofing on a waterfront property" loading="lazy"><figcaption>OUR OWN WORK &middot; METAL ROOFING ON THE WATER</figcaption></figure>
    <div class="pull rv d3">Same age, same installer, same material. The only variable is which way the building faces.<span class="attr">From the 15 July inspection</span></div>
  </div>
</section><section id="story">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="rail rv"><span>0 3 &middot; The Family</span><span class="mid">&middot;</span><span class="t2">Tarpon Springs since 1990</span></div>
    <h1 class="h1 rv d1">Thirty-six years, five counties.</h1>
    <p class="sub rv d1">Who you are actually hiring, and how we work on a property that has guests in it.</p>
    <p class="body rv d2">Arry&rsquo;s Roofing is family owned and has worked out of the same address on East Spruce Street in Tarpon Springs since 1990. Five counties, in this order: Pinellas, Hillsborough, Manatee, Sarasota, Pasco. Longboat Key is the middle of that map, not the edge of it.</p><p class="body rv d2">The crews are employees, not subcontractors. The same people who tear off your roof are the people who close it in that evening. We run our own sheet metal shop and custom shape the components that have to fit a building exactly, which on cottages of this age is most of the perimeter.</p><p class="body rv d2">Coastal work is the bulk of what we do, so the fastener and alloy questions in this document are not theoretical for us. They are the reason we keep going back to certain products on the water and away from others.</p>
    <div class="statblock rv d2"><div class="sb"><div class="n">1990</div><div class="l">Family owned since</div></div><div class="sb"><div class="n">5<small>counties</small></div><div class="l">Pinellas to Pasco</div></div><div class="sb"><div class="n">0</div><div class="l">Subcontractors on your roof</div></div><div class="sb"><div class="n">8<small>units</small></div><div class="l">Rental units in scope</div></div></div>
    <ol class="numlist rv d3"><li><span class="num">0 1</span><h3>We phase around your bookings, not around ours.</h3><p>Six buildings with eight rental units is a scheduling problem a house does not have. We sequence the buildings so occupied units keep a closed roof every night, and we agree the order with you before anything comes off.</p></li><li><span class="num">0 2</span><h3>Nothing gets opened up further than we can dry in the same day.</h3><p>On a barrier island the afternoon weather is not a hypothetical. Tear-off is sized to what the crew can get back watertight before they leave.</p></li><li><span class="num">0 3</span><h3>The wood repairs are priced with the roof, not discovered during it.</h3><p>The eave rot and the decking at Beachside Cottage #2 are already in the numbers below. That is the line item most often left as an allowance, and it is the one that turns a fixed price into an open one.</p></li><li><span class="num">0 4</span><h3>One crew, one shop, one point of contact.</h3><p>You call me. Not a call centre, and not a different sub for the metal, the wood and the flashings.</p></li></ol>
    <div class="tile rv d3"><div class="eyebrow">Our promise</div><div class="th">Permits, dump fees, workers&rsquo; compensation, general liability and sales tax are inside every number in this document. Nothing about this job arrives as an extra afterwards.</div></div>
  </div>
</section><section id="findings" class="dark">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="rail rv"><span>0 4 &middot; The Inspection</span><span class="mid">&middot;</span><span class="t2">Inspected 15 July 2026</span></div>
    <h1 class="h1 rv d1">What the roofs are actually doing.</h1>
    <p class="sub rv d1">Six Gulf-side buildings, eight rental units, and one cottage in noticeably worse shape than the rest.</p>
    <p class="body rv d2">Everything below is what I saw on the roof on the day, and what each condition means for the buildings underneath. No condition here is listed to pad the report. Where something is fine, I have said it is fine.</p><p class="body rv d2">The existing Gulf-side system is 5V-Crimp exposed-fastener galvanized steel. That matters for almost everything that follows, so it is worth stating plainly at the top: every panel on those roofs is screwed on through the face of the metal.</p>
    <ol class="cardstack rv d2"><li><span class="num">0 1</span><h3>Widespread corrosion throughout the Gulf-side roof panels, with rust through the eave trim, the rake trim and the perimeter metal.</h3><p>Galvanized steel defends itself with a zinc coating, and salt air spends every day wearing that coating away. Once it is gone there is nothing between the weather and bare steel, and the corrosion accelerates rather than holding steady.</p></li><li><span class="num">0 2</span><h3>Aging exposed fasteners showing signs of corrosion, and moisture intrusion around the roof edges.</h3><p>On a 5V-Crimp roof every panel is fastened through its face, so each screw is a hole held shut by a rubber washer. Across six buildings that is thousands of them, and a corroded fastener in a hardened washer is where the water starts.</p></li><li><span class="num">0 3</span><h3>Soft and deteriorated wood along portions of the eaves, where water has already penetrated beneath the roofing system.</h3><p>This is where it stops being a roofing problem. Rotted eave framing has nothing solid left to hold a fastener, so it has to be cut out and replaced before any new roof can be fixed down over it.</p></li><li><span class="num">0 4</span><h3>Most pipe boots, vent flashings and roof penetrations show age-related deterioration: failed sealants, cracked and weathered boots, corroded vent collars, damaged flashing.</h3><p>Penetrations almost always leak before panels do. With this many of them in the same condition there is no sense repairing them one at a time. They should be replaced with the roof so the whole assembly is the same age from that day forward.</p></li><li><span class="num">0 5</span><h3>Several buildings on the south side carry previous storm repairs, where replacement panel sections were grafted into the existing roof.</h3><p>Those grafts did their job at the time and stopped the immediate damage. They are patches in an old system rather than a repair of it, so until the roofs are replaced they are the spots worth watching after a hard blow.</p></li><li><span class="num">0 6</span><h3>Beachside Cottage #2 is missing both its front and rear fascia boards, leaving framing exposed and areas of roof decking deteriorated.</h3><p>This is the worst condition on the property. With the fascia gone, wind-driven rain and salt spray have been running straight into the open roof edge for some time. The decking repairs here belong inside the replacement project.</p></li><li><span class="num">0 7</span><h3>The bayside buildings are in good overall condition and need only routine preventative maintenance.</h3><p>Same age, same original roofing, and they are fine. The difference is that the cottages in front of them take the salt first. That single comparison is the clearest evidence there is that exposure and not age has been eating the Gulf-side roofs.</p></li></ol>
  </div>
</section><section id="product-5">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="rail rv"><span>0 5 &middot; </span><span class="mid">&middot;</span><span class="t2">Exposed fastener steel</span></div>
    <h1 class="h1 rv d1">Replace like for like.</h1>
    <p class="sub rv d1">New metal, new wood, and the same system the buildings have now.</p>
    <div class="cols"><div><p class="body rv d2">5V-Crimp is the old Florida panel. Double ribs at each panel edge create the profile and conceal an anti-siphon channel, at 24&Prime; net coverage. Gulf Coast Supply lists it in 26 and 24 gauge steel and in 0.032 aluminium.</p><p class="body rv d2">Every panel is fastened through its face. That is what makes it the least expensive of the three to install. It is also the exact condition your current roofs have failed under. New screws and new washers reset the clock without changing the design.</p><p class="body rv d2">Choose this if the priority is sound, watertight roofs over the eight rental units at the lowest number, accepting a shorter interval before this conversation comes round again.</p><figure class="shot rv d2"><img src="https://arrysroofing.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/Rowland1.jpg" alt="A metal roof installed by Arry&rsquo;s Roofing" loading="lazy"><figcaption>OUR OWN WORK &middot; METAL ROOFING</figcaption></figure></div><div class="panel rv d2"><div class="eyebrow">5V-Crimp exposed fastener</div><dl class="dl"><div><dt>Panel profile</dt><dd>5V-Crimp, 24&Prime; net coverage</dd></div><div><dt>Fastening</dt><dd class="hi">Exposed, through the panel face</dd></div><div><dt>Material</dt><dd>26 and 24 gauge steel; 0.032 aluminium also available</dd></div><div><dt>Substructure</dt><dd>15/32&Prime; minimum plywood, or 1&Prime; x 4&Prime; SYP purlins on plywood</dd></div><div><dt>Minimum slope</dt><dd>3:12</dd></div><div><dt>Relief options</dt><dd>Flat, mini rib or striated</dd></div><div><dt>Substrate and finish warranty</dt><dd>AZ-50 premium paint 50/30 year; AZ-50 standard paint 40/30 year; AZ-55 unpainted mill finish 25 year</dd></div><div><dt>Florida product approval</dt><dd>FL34538.08-R3 (HVHZ), FL33818.08-R5 (non-HVHZ)</dd></div><div><dt>Miami-Dade NOA</dt><dd>24-0625.01</dd></div><div><dt>Coastal rust warranty</dt><dd class="hi">None &mdash; this is not a coastal-specific product</dd></div><div><dt>Replaces</dt><dd>The existing 5V-Crimp galvanized steel roofs</dd></div></dl><div class="note">Panel and warranty data read from Gulf Coast Supply&rsquo;s published 5V Crimp specification, August 2026. Paint warranties cover the finish only; the substrate is warranted separately.</div></div></div>
    
    <div class="statblock rv d2"><div class="sb"><div class="n">24<small>&Prime;</small></div><div class="l">Net coverage per panel</div></div><div class="sb"><div class="n">26/24<small>ga</small></div><div class="l">Steel gauge options</div></div><div class="sb"><div class="n">3:12</div><div class="l">Minimum slope</div></div><div class="sb"><div class="n">35<small>+</small></div><div class="l">Colours, 20 of them PVDF</div></div></div>
    
    
    <p class="body rv d3">I will not dress this option up. It buys you sound roofs, new wood and a closed building envelope, at the lowest of the three numbers. It does not change the reason the last roofs failed.</p>
  </div>
</section><section id="scope-6" class="dark">
