    <ol class="cardstack rv d2"><li class="shared"><span class="num">0 1</span><span class="tag shared">Same in every option</span><h3>Remove the existing 5V-Crimp exposed-fastener galvanized panels, perimeter trim and fasteners from all six Gulf-side buildings, down to the deck.</h3><p>Nothing new goes over the top of a corroded roof. Everything comes off so the decking underneath can actually be looked at.</p></li><li class="shared"><span class="num">0 2</span><span class="tag shared">Same in every option</span><h3>Replace soft and deteriorated wood along the eaves, and deteriorated roof decking, including the areas identified at Beachside Cottage #2.</h3><p>Rotted framing has nothing solid left to hold a fastener. This is the work that has to happen before any panel system can be fixed down over it.</p></li><li class="shared"><span class="num">0 3</span><span class="tag shared">Same in every option</span><h3>Rebuild the missing front and rear fascia boards at Beachside Cottage #2.</h3><p>With the fascia gone, the roof edge has been open to wind-driven rain and salt spray. It gets closed as part of the roof, not as a call-back afterwards.</p></li><li class="shared"><span class="num">0 4</span><span class="tag shared">Same in every option</span><h3>Replace all pipe boots, vent flashings, vent collars and penetration flashings.</h3><p>Penetrations leak before panels do. Replacing them with the roof means the whole assembly is the same age from that day on.</p></li><li class="shared"><span class="num">0 5</span><span class="tag shared">Same in every option</span><h3>New eave trim, rake trim and perimeter metal to match the panel system.</h3><p>On a concealed-fastener roof the perimeter is where the fastening actually lives, so the trim is doing structural work rather than finishing work.</p></li><li class="only"><span class="num">0 6</span><span class="tag only">Only in this option</span><h3>Install CoastalMAX&reg; zinc-aluminium-magnesium alloy panels in a concealed